title: Mind_Maze_D1
position: 12,12
---
<<declare $MindDone1 = false>>
<<declare $MindDone2 = false>>
<<declare $MindDone3 = false>>
<<declare $MindDone4 = false>>
<<declare $MindDone5 = false>>

<<declare $MindTotalWeight = 0>>
<<declare $MindRoll = 0>>
<<declare $MindW1 = 0>>
<<declare $MindW2 = 0>>
<<declare $MindW3 = 0>>
<<declare $MindW4 = 0>>
<<declare $MindW5 = 0>>
===

title: MindMaze_OpenScene
---
I: Every working hour requires me to say things I did not entirely believe.
I: Apart from earning enough to live on,
I: what else did they leave me with...?
<<pause_dialogue>>
“I”: [b]It's all right. Let's go home.[/b]
<<jump MindMaze_Start>>
===

title: MindMaze_Start
---
// Failure route: enter episode five.
“I”: Let's see who [b][u]called to me[/u][/b] today.
<<if not $JushiSuccess>>
    <<if not $MindDone5>>
        <<jump MindMaze_5>>
    <<else>>
        <<jump MindMaze_End>>
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

// Success route: enter weighted random pool.
<<jump MindMaze_Roll>>
===


title: MindMaze_Roll
---
<<set $MindW1 to 0>>
<<set $MindW2 to 0>>
<<set $MindW3 to 0>>
<<set $MindW4 to 0>>

// Accessible after success only.

// Episode 1 unlocks after success.
<<if not $MindDone1>>
    <<set $MindW1 to 1>>
<<endif>>

// Episode 2 requires the heart-to-heart flag.
<<if $LikeU and not $MindDone2>>
    <<set $MindW2 to 1>>
<<endif>>

// Episodes 3 and 4 have higher weights.
<<if not $MindDone3>>
    <<set $MindW3 to 3>>
<<endif>>

<<if not $MindDone4>>
    <<set $MindW4 to 3>>
<<endif>>

<<set $MindTotalWeight to $MindW1 + $MindW2 + $MindW3 + $MindW4>>

<<if $MindTotalWeight == 0>>
    <<jump MindMaze_End>>
<<endif>>

<<set $MindRoll to dice($MindTotalWeight)>>

<<if $MindRoll <= $MindW1>>
    <<jump MindMaze_1>>
<<elseif $MindRoll <= $MindW1 + $MindW2>>
    <<jump MindMaze_2>>
<<elseif $MindRoll <= $MindW1 + $MindW2 + $MindW3>>
    <<jump MindMaze_3>>
<<else>>
    <<jump MindMaze_4>>
<<endif>>
===


title: MindMaze_Continue
---
// The failure route never enters the success pool.
<<if not $JushiSuccess>>
    <<jump MindMaze_End>>
<<endif>>

// All unlocked episodes have been completed.
<<if $MindDone1 and $MindDone3 and $MindDone4 and (not $LikeU or $MindDone2)>>
    <<jump MindMaze_End>>
<<endif>>

-> Keep talking.
    “I”: Who else [b][u]called to me[/u][/b] today?
    <<jump MindMaze_Roll>>
-> Mm...
    <<jump MindMaze_End>>
===


title: MindMaze_1
---
<<mindmaze_roll_anim 1>>
<<set_speakers "宝钗, 吕蒙, 柯湘">>
// Episode 1: Baochai, Lv Meng and Ke Xiang discuss openness toward differing perspectives; unlocked after sale.
Baochai: [i]I wipe away a hero's tears and leave the scholar's home. [/i]
Baochai: [i]I thank compassion and take the tonsure beneath the lotus throne.[/i]
Baochai: [i]No bond was fated to remain; in an instant we part. [/i]
Baochai: [i]Naked we came, and naked we go, with nothing to hold us fast.[/i]
Baochai: [i]Where shall I seek a rain cape and a traveler's hat? [/i]
Baochai: [i]With straw sandals and a begging bowl, I shall wander wherever fate leads.[/i]
“I”: My precious elder sister, how am I to describe the feeling that stranger left me with?
Baochai: Since she treated you kindly, why should you not return her kindness?
“I”: But the Buddhist teachings she spoke of aren't words I know well enough to understand, let alone use so freely.
Baochai: If she wishes to converse, you may answer her with neither servility nor arrogance. 
Baochai: Only, there's no need to think so little of yourself.
Baochai: For all we know, she [b]has not yet quite attained the understanding she speaks of[/b]. 
Baochai: Perhaps that is why she has so much to say.
Baochai: Whether your counter desk gathers dust or holds flowers and fine objects, 
Baochai: surely neither should hinder the cultivation of her mind?
Baochai: If she claims no attachment to things, 
Baochai: why should furnishings and formal courtesies stir up such distinctions in her heart?
Baochai: If she truly wished to cut her worldly ties, 
Baochai: a modest gift her friend would cherish should have sufficed.
Baochai: Why call upon old acquaintances in the municipal education bureau and certain companies to announce her arrival, 
Baochai: and have everything arranged like a business reception?
Baochai: She might perfectly well have dealt with you as a customer with a shop assistant.
Baochai: Why bring in yet another bond: a family friend of your employer, 
Baochai: attended upon by someone in that household's service?
Baochai: And at the end of it all, 
Baochai: she told you where her son studies, and what degree he is pursuing.
Baochai: Naturally, he is dear to her heart. 
Baochai: But what have these worldly distinctions to do with you?
Baochai: [b]You see, the ties of this world are not so easily laid aside merely by saying one has let them go.[/b]
“I”: Yet is a courteous nod between acquaintances, or a simple reciprocity bounded by contract,
“I”: necessarily the best way to encounter another person?
“I”: She talked to me at such length. Perhaps, like the young people marching in the streets or the monks preaching their faith,
“I”: she was trying to imagine some new possibility from within the social relationships and ideas she already knew.
“I”: But does she know that I don't entirely agree with her, 
"I": or even understand all the Buddhist ideas she invokes?
“I”: Does she care what I think, or how our thoughts differ?
“I”: Or did she only want someone willing to listen?
Lv Meng: Well, I reckon people learn to read and pick up a trade because they want a better life.
Lv Meng: For themselves and for those around them.
Lv Meng: As for what the world should look like in the end...
Lv Meng: Communism, the Pure Land, a world of Great Unity...
Lv Meng: or that welfare state the social democrats talk about these days?
Lv Meng: [b]I honestly don't know[/b]
Lv Meng: Can people really live in harmony while keeping their differences? 
Lv Meng: I don't know that either.
Lv Meng: Some look out for the poor; 
Lv Meng: some want local communities to govern their own affairs.
Lv Meng: Others speak up for women, or for people nobody much cares to hear.
Lv Meng: Their arguments needn't agree, and neither do their motives.
Lv Meng: Some want to save the world, some genuinely have a bodhisattva's heart,
Lv Meng: And some just want their names in lights.
Lv Meng: Still, any real, tangible good done is worth cherishing. 
Lv Meng: [b]Nobody has a monopoly on the truth[/b]
Lv Meng: Meet them. Talk with them. Understand them—or don't, if you can't.
Lv Meng: Whether they'll listen to you is something you can only learn by spending time together.
Lv Meng: [b]You needn't agree on every point, nor make every conversation a contest.[/b]
Lv Meng: Learn where people are coming from and how they reached their views. 
Lv Meng: Then feel out the stones and decide how you'll cross the river yourself.
Baochai: [i]What is desirable is called good;[/i] 
Baochai: [i]what one possesses within oneself is called trustworthy; [/i] 
Baochai: [i]what is full and complete is called beautiful.[/i] 
Ke Xiang: The Chairman said the story of Lv Meng was essential reading for the Red Army.
(I feel her straighten my collar.) 
Ke Xiang: And Baochai stands at the very summit of China's greatest classical novel.
“I”: (I nod.)
Ke Xiang: But every age has its bounds. 
Ke Xiang: No learning, however sound, can encompass the whole ground.
Ke Xiang: We're shaped by the relations in which we live,
Ke Xiang: and by convictions we did not wholly choose to give.
Ke Xiang: You've read so many books, walked so many roads. 
Ke Xiang: Will you leave others to decide where your own road goes?
Ke Xiang: Look closely, think again: [b]where did these ideas begin, and whose purposes do they serve?[/b]
Ke Xiang: Which hold a truth you'll carry all your days?
Ke Xiang: Which bear the speaker's ideology, and must be weighed in other ways?
Ke Xiang: Which might help, but have their light obscured by how they're told?
Ke Xiang: Which sound natural, yet speak from positions language itself has made and moulded?
Ke Xiang: Go beyond the people who offer counsel. Only then will you reach the places they never could.
“I”: [b]And you? Will you answer every question I put to you?[/b]
(Ke Xiang falls silent.)
<<set $MindDone1 to true>>
<<mindmaze_reset_hands>>
<<jump MindMaze_Continue>>
===

title: MindMaze_2
---
<<mindmaze_roll_anim 2>>
<<set_speakers "柯湘, 吕蒙, 赫尔墨斯, 宝钗">>
// Episode 2: Ke Xiang, Lv Meng, Hermes and Baochai; unlocked by heart-to-heart.
“I”: I've actually been looking forward to talking with you.
“I”: I so want you to tell me what belief is, what Buddhist thought is, what religion is.
”I“: You know, from [b]your[/b] perspectives. 
“I”: And how the [i]united front against Japanese Militarist Invasion[/i], 
“I”: the [i]national forms[/i] of the 1950s,
“I”: and the [i]transformations of the economic base[/i] in the 1960s and 1970s shaped Chinese Buddhism.
Ke Xiang: Child, you've already thought of economics, politics, culture, and social relations.
Ke Xiang: A promising way to proceed--you've learned to question the conditions.
Ke Xiang: But if Buddhism is what you wish to understand,
Ke Xiang: You must look beyond the China you know, to the place where it first took its stand.
Ke Xiang: How did it arise in ancient India, and what was life like in those days?
Ke Xiang: How did the monastic community take form, and what sustained its daily life?
Ke Xiang: What needs and obligations held it together amid the world's disputes and strife?
Ke Xiang: Why did the Mauryan court extend its favour, and kings provide it with support?
Ke Xiang: How did Buddhists debate, compete, and change alongside Brahmanical thought?
Ke Xiang: How did monasteries, stupas, and images spread, until worshippers came from far and wide?
Ke Xiang: How did Greek and West Asian images give Siddhartha a body to behold?
Ke Xiang: How did those forms reach China, where new faces and features would unfold?
Ke Xiang: Through wars and trade, through peoples meeting, through dynasties rising and falling,
Ke Xiang: How did teachings change their course, answering each new age's calling?
Ke Xiang: And now you ask of the Northern Expedition, urbanisation, and the struggle against fascism;
Ke Xiang: Of the Liberation War and New China, and their relations to Buddhism.
Ke Xiang: First, read. Go out and explore. Let what you find become the ground.
Ke Xiang: Only then can we discuss what lies beneath the claims that you've found.
Ke Xiang: [b]When accounts disagree, don't rush to believe what you first hear.[/b]
Ke Xiang: [b]No account stands outside its time; no position is free of what it holds dear.[/b]
Ke Xiang: Ask what evidence supports each claim, whose interests it may serve.
Ke Xiang: Ask what perspective gives it shape, and what its telling may preserve.
Ke Xiang: Perhaps then you'll trace these histories through modern China's bodily culture and national forms,
Ke Xiang: And we'll speak of Emily Wilcox, Laurence Coderre, and Chinese opera's transformation—
Ke Xiang: Matters closer to my own life, and worthy of our shared investigation.
“I”: ...Ah. So you're not going to tell me.
“I”: [i]Huwei General[/i], will you teach me instead?
“I”: Please tell me how Buddhism relates to the great clans, divided territories, wars, and exchanges among peoples.
Lv Meng: Well now... my girl,
Lv Meng: I could tell you about the shamans and wandering masters of [i]Jiangdong[/i],
Lv Meng: or how [i]Kang Senghui[/i] brought Buddhist teachings to Jianye  
Lv Meng: and how [i]His Most Respected Majesty[/i] (refers to Sun Quan) built him a monastery.
Lv Meng: I could even tell you a curious tale of young [i]Sun Xiaolian[/i] (refers to Sun Quan), fifteen years old, 
Lv Meng: accompanying the Dowager to [i]Chaoyin Temple[/i] and teaching the people through Buddhist wonders.
Lv Meng: But if you ask whether that had to do with governing the people of Jiangdong, 
Lv Meng: with resisting or drawing in the local great families,
Lv Meng: or with the exchanges between Jiangdong and the Central Plains...
Lv Meng: Well, I only came to live in your head because we read the [i]Records of the Three Kingdoms[/i], 
Lv Meng: [i]Wu Slips and Wu Histories[/i], and [i]Peaks and Valleys [/i]together.
Lv Meng: [b]If the question lies beyond what you know, I cannot answer it,[/b]
Lv Meng: [b]whether the [i]Marquis of Chanling[/i] himself would have known or not.[/b]
“Hermes”: We can read new things together, sweetie. 
“Hermes”: And don't fret about biting off more than you can chew.
“Hermes”: Haven't we been to the [i]White Horse Temple[/i] and the [i]Longmen Grottoes[/i] in the north,
“Hermes”: and [i]Chaoyin Temple, Dajue Temple, Lingshan,[/i] and [i]Qixia Mountain[/i] in the south?
“Hermes”: We've read about movements that honoured or suppressed Buddhism, 
“Hermes”: along with the economic conditions, popular mobilisations, social organisations, and political struggles that made them possible.
“Hermes”: [b]Why should any of this be beyond our ability to learn and take in?[/b]
“Hermes”: Leaving university hasn't deprived you of the right to learn, has it?
Lv Meng: Quite right. 
Lv Meng: You don't need to research everything down to the last footnote as you do with East Asian cultural studies.
Lv Meng: Start with a film or a documentary if you like.
Lv Meng:  Take it a little at a time. Read widely, and let the whole of Asia come into view.
“I”: My precious elder sister...
Baochai: Let me ask you something first. What is [i]emptiness[/i]?
-> Change.
    Baochai: Yes. A notion you've known for quite some time.
    Baochai: But [i]emptiness[/i] cannot be exhausted by the thought that all things change.
    Baochai: It asks whether anything possesses an independent, unchanging [i]self-nature[/i].
    Baochai: You already knew something of [i]dependent origination[/i], of relation and change.
    Baochai: Why, then, upon hearing some unfamiliar terms today,
    Baochai: must you immediately determine what they have to do with your own life?

-> Nothingness.
    Baochai: You see, [i]emptiness[/i] does not mean that nothing exists.
    Baochai: Things arise through causes and conditions. They have no independent, immutable [i]self-nature[/i], but that does not make them mere nothingness.
    Baochai: You've heard so many unfamiliar accounts today that you are eager to locate yourself in relation to them.
    Baochai: Yet you have scarcely had time to consider what each one actually means.

Baochai: You studied philosophy before, and wondered how knowledge enters into relation with your own experience.
Baochai: [b]Knowing something and understanding why it matters to you are, after all, two different things.[/b]
“Hermes”: Didn't we read [i]Merleau-Ponty[/i] and [i]Husserl[/i] together?
“Hermes”: Remember that passage in [i]The Primacy of Perception[/i]—
“Hermes”: the [i]paradox of immanence and transcendence[/i]?
“Hermes”: Imagine the purple clay teapot before you. 
“Hermes”: You can see its front, yet you know it also has a back and sides,
“Hermes”: and tea leaves inside you cannot now see. 
“Hermes”: [b]Scenes of handling, using, and exchanging it are absent from this instant.[/b]
“Hermes”: What you perceive is one aspect.
“Hermes”: [b]What you experience has never been confined to that aspect alone.[/b]
“Hermes”: For Merleau-Ponty, perception is [b]immanent[/b] insofar as what is perceived appears to the one who perceives;
“Hermes”: and [b]transcendent[/b] insofar as it always exceeds what is immediately given.
“Hermes”: We've glimpsed Buddhism before, even if we only wandered through one small corner of it.
“Hermes”: And you know that [b]the histories, ideas, and social relations we have yet to encounter do not cease to exist because we have not seen them.[/b]
“Hermes”: But isn't it funny? 
“Hermes”: That knowledge was there all along.
“Hermes”: Why is it only today, after listening to Hou, 
“Hermes”: [b]that you suddenly want to pick up the whole teapot and turn it around in your hands?[/b]
Baochai: Are you so eager to understand Buddhism because you wish to study it,
Baochai: or because [b]something about today's conversation[/b] makes you feel you must understand it at once?

// Original placeholder: insert the rest of episode two here.
// Preserve the two choices answering what emptiness means.
// Both choices rejoin at the common ending.
<<set $MindDone2 to true>>
<<mindmaze_reset_hands>>
<<jump MindMaze_Continue>>
===

title: MindMaze_3
---
<<mindmaze_roll_anim 3>>
<<set_speakers "MBTI, 伍尔夫, 宝钗">>
// Episode 3: MBTI, Woolf and Baochai; always unlocked.
MBTI: Babe, are you exhausted? 
MBTI: Do you need a little break?
MBTI: All that emotional labour, all those 'mm-hm, mm-hm's'—that is so draining, right?
MBTI: Of course, you are absolutely entitled to end those conversations.
MBTI: But did your boss, your customer, or, 
MBTI: frankly, [b]the job itself give you that option?[/b]
MBTI: And was anyone meeting you halfway in that conversation? Anyone?
MBTI: Did all that [b]education really just buy you the ability to keep awkward silences from happening?[/b]
Woolf: My dear, after two hours surrendered to these little exertions of sympathy,
Woolf: what remains of the self that was yours before they began?
Woolf: Did you enjoy the things you said? 
Woolf: Did you believe them? 
Woolf: Or has the [b]person you suppose yourself to be been submerged beneath the obligation to please?[/b]
Woolf: Do you remember [i]Mrs Ramsay[/i]?
Woolf: So perceptive a woman, so gifted at sensing a room and putting others at ease.
Woolf: She read Shakespeare. Her memory was extraordinary.
Woolf: yet all those gifts were poured away in conversation after conversation 
Woolf: restoring Mr. Ramsay's faith in himself as a philosopher,
Woolf: in evening after evening spent making her family and every guest feel effortlessly at ease.
Woolf: But such conversations are not paintings. 
Woolf: They cannot be kept upon a wall, or rescued with a fresh coat of varnish.
Woolf: Mrs Ramsay's pleasures and decisions, the work of her attention,
Woolf: the evenings and meals made agreeable by her presence...
Woolf: --[b]all pass like wind when the voices die away.[/b]
Woolf: Have you, my friend, 
Woolf: [b]have you wedded yourself to your own trivial trade, [/b]
Woolf: [b]racking your brain again and again to furnish the right reply[/b]
Woolf: Only the tiniest trickle of the money spun from the expenditure of your own voice ever reaches your pocket.
Woolf: Your thoughts may endure less long than a single teapot you have handed across the counter.
Baochai: Yet did you not live through those very hours yourself?
Baochai: From one change to another, [b]who among us—and in what form—can ever be kept forever?[/b]
Baochai: You were present in every moment, every exchange.
Baochai: distaste, evasion, flattery, ease, pleasure, sudden understanding, quiet observation...
Baochai: [b]all of it was part of your life.[/b]
Baochai: It is neither wholly stolen from you by others, nor wholly slipped away through time's fingers.
Baochai: [i]Could all fair things not fall into the passing stream?[/i]
Baochai: [i]Could one blossom not lie in fragrant dust? [/i]
<<set $MindDone3 to true>>
<<mindmaze_reset_hands>>
<<jump MindMaze_Continue>>
===

title: MindMaze_4
---
<<mindmaze_roll_anim 4>>
<<set_speakers "名妓, 比尔博, 赫尔墨斯, 伍尔夫, 宝钗, 柯湘">>
// Episode 4: Late Ming Courtesan, Bilbo, Hermes and others; always unlocked.
Late Ming Courtesan: Once Zhang Dai acquired the [i]Wooden Dragon, [/i]
Late Ming Courtesan: he gathered his Dingchou Poetry Society and 
Late Ming Courtesan: [i]诸公赐之名，并赋小言咏之。[/i]
(Whaaaaat? Chinese Classics? Hard to read...)
Late Ming Courtesan: Master Zhang spared neither ink nor paper, 
Late Ming Courtesan: [i]记满座高朋之题咏，诸名盈帙；[/i]
Late Ming Courtesan: Nor did he spare a hundred taels in conveyance, that he might 
Late Ming Courtesan: [i]磨其尺木，勒铭曰：秋涛夏云。[/i]
“Hermes”: That's Zhang Dai's [i]The Wooden Dragon[/i], by a survivor of the late Ming. 
“Hermes”: We've read it before, when we were studying collecting and connoisseurship among Ming and Qing gentlemen.
“I”: (read read read read.)
"Bilbo": [b]My dear child[/b], why such a furrowed brow? 
("Bilbo" rubs the crease between “I”’s eyebrows with his thumb)
"Bilbo": A young lady with a frown like that? 
"Bilbo": You'll have three little furrows there before you know it!
"Bilbo": Why, people will think you're terribly stern before you've even said a word.
"Bilbo": Has it been so long since you read classical Chinese that the words won't come to you?
"Bilbo": Poor dear. You can't do as we figures of your imagination do 
"Bilbo"" and [b]slip straight past the language to its meaning.[/b]
"Bilbo": Well, Zhang Dai is telling us about a most peculiar piece of old wood.
"Bilbo": It passed through the collection of an honoured servant of the early Ming, 
"Bilbo": a fire, and the hands of antique dealers,
"Bilbo": through the visits of noble families, inheritance within households, 
"Bilbo": and even the upheaval of a change of dynasty.
"Bilbo": It wasn't merely the timber itself that mattered. 
"Bilbo": [b]There were the people through whose hands it travelled,[/b]
"Bilbo": [b]their friendships, and the stories and marks of distinction that those friends knew how to recognise.[/b]
"Bilbo": That's why someone thought it worth seventeen rhinoceros-horn cups in exchange, if you can imagine!
"Bilbo": Rather like the [i]mithril coat [/i]I gave my nephew.
"Bilbo": It carries something of our family's wealth and our adventures,
"Bilbo": and hints at his uncle's—heh, that would be me—acquaintance with the royal house of the Dwarves.
"Bilbo": Why, it might even call to mind the decline of Durin's line and the long quest to reclaim their kingdom.
"Bilbo": But the thing must be worn, handled, and put to use—and, most importantly,
"Bilbo": seen by a discerning friend or fellow traveller—before all those meanings can truly come into their own.
"Bilbo": And when my old friend Thorin gave it to me,
"Bilbo": he was not simply preserving his memory, or showing what he felt towards me.
"Bilbo": He placed a wish for my safety, and the affection between us, into the gift itself.
Baochai: And it is through all these passages from hand to hand that the piece of stubborn wood acquired its meaning.
Baochai: Zhang Dai says his father once intended it for Prince Lu, 
Baochai: but inadvertently wrote the forbidden words 'Wooden Dragon' and so lost the chance to present it.
Baochai: That reminds me of the coffin my brother sent to the [i]Eastern Mansion[/i].
Baochai: It had originally been meant for the late [i]Prince Yizhong[/i]. 
Baochai: When it was not used, it found its way instead to Cousin Zhen.
Baochai: One object was fit to be presented at court, yet could not fulfil that destiny.
Baochai: [b]Once you begin to tell these histories, the objects almost seem able to speak for themselves.[/b]
“Hermes”: Actually, connoisseurship is already an activity with rules, boundaries, and established procedures.
“Hermes”: Especially at those [b]semi-public gatherings of refined company,[/b]
“Hermes”: guests are admitted because their standing or their emotional ties have already been recognised.
“Hermes”: So being invited is itself an enactment of the rules of the game.
“Hermes”: [i]Connoisseurship[/i] isn't just standing there staring at something, you know.
“Hermes”: [b]It's an embodied, sensory encounter with objects, inseparable from social practice.[/b]
“Hermes”: An object almost seems to ask us to respond in a particular way.
“Hermes”: A coffin holds the dead in a funeral
“Hermes”: mithril is worn against the body
“Hermes”: the Wooden Dragon is displayed and admired, 
“Hermes”: while the [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i][/b] is used for tea or display in a scholar's study.
“Hermes”: But none of those uses is neutral.
“Hermes”: [b]The object implies which practices and users are considered appropriate within a given society.[/b]
“Hermes”: It asks the person who takes it up to perform the role that society has already imagined for that user.
Baochai: People lend objects the dignity of their station, and objects lend people a greater name. How many relations between people must grow out of that!
“Hermes”: Exactly, Sister Bao!
“Hermes”: You've just reminded me of Jonathan Hay's [i]Sensuous Surfaces:[/i]
“Hermes”: [i]The Decorative Object in Early Modern China[/i].
“Hermes”: Take the funeral at your Eastern Mansion.
“Hermes”: In that setting the coffin already comes with something like a set of instructions:
“Hermes”: to hold the body at the funeral, gather people from the deceased's social world, and make burial a socially recognised act.
“Hermes”: [b]But I think the more important point is that the same object demands different things of different people.[/b]
“Hermes”: Merchant relatives offer up a treasure to demonstrate its wealth and its former intimacy with the powerful.
“Hermes”: Jia Zhen, at the Eastern Mansion, acquires the coffin and weeps, performing his grief for the dead.
“Hermes”: And the servants? 
“Hermes”: They hoist the coffin onto their shoulders and haul it where it has to go.
“Hermes”: [b]The object's call, then, operates as a system in its own right.[/b]
“Hermes”: Through objects, people are woven into the fabric of social relations.
“Hermes”: Before a piece like Wood Like a Dragon, 
“Hermes”: the host throws a banquet and plays the cultured scion of an illustrious line with far-flung connections.
“Hermes”: The literati cite the classics, bestow names, compose verses,
“Hermes”: playing the erudite, refined men of letters.
Late Ming Courtesan: If that is as far as your thoughts reach, 
Late Ming Courtesan: it only proves how [b]comfortably detached this little critique of yours is.[/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: And what of me?
Late Ming Courtesan: Invited to the table, I must sing well, speak wittily, 
Late Ming Courtesan: and know when to laugh, when to answer, and when to hold my tongue.
Late Ming Courtesan: My profession demands that I know how to judge the gentlemen's paintings, 
Late Ming Courtesan: scholars' calligraphy, desk curios, and essays...
Late Ming Courtesan: I have to comment gracefully, compose verses in reply, offer praises...
Late Ming Courtesan: and make every last one of them believe I am [b]his kindred spirit.[/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: [b]But while they are admiring those objects, someone is also admiring me as an object.[/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: I am like that lump of wood. And I am not. Not at all.
Late Ming Courtesan: The moment I cease to perform the part—
Late Ming Courtesan: no more singing, no more entertaining, no longer the charming woman, 
Late Ming Courtesan: that understanding companion in their eyes—
Late Ming Courtesan: where on earth am I left to stand?
Late Ming Courtesan: Lose one curio, and there is always the next.
Late Ming Courtesan: End one elegant gathering, and another takes its place.
Late Ming Courtesan: Weaving the same comfortable net to hold all of you just as neatly.
Late Ming Courtesan: But if I can no longer play my allotted role inside it, 
Late Ming Courtesan: [b]will that net still have a knot for me? [/b]
Woolf: I can well imagine your gentlemen turning those antiques and treasures over in their palms.
Woolf: The clay, the shape, the signature, the provenance of a purple clay teapot;
Woolf: Then you—your accomplishments, your family, your beauty, your lovers.
Woolf: My dear, beyond all that, 
Woolf: how are you any different from the refined gentlemen appraising you at that very table?
Woolf: [b]Same person, just a different sex.[/b]
Woolf: Yet the treatment each of you receives is worlds apart.
Late Ming Courtesan: There are [b]other differences[/b].
Late Ming Courtesan: Different origins, different access to education,
Late Ming Courtesan: different knowledge we were ever to acquire along the way.
Late Ming Courtesan: Yet there we sit at the same table, 
Late Ming Courtesan: judging the same objects， appraising the same trinket to trade in cultural capital，
Late Ming Courtesan: though we reached it by different roads.
Late Ming Courtesan: Truly!
Late Ming Courtesan: [b][i]Gongchun Teapot[/i], hear I question you![/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: How much of your renown comes from the labour of the hands that made you,
Late Ming Courtesan: [b]from generations of experience and oral knowledge passed among those whose room to live was so painfully narrow?[/b]
Late Ming Late Ming Courtesan: [b]And how much from the gentlemen who later named you, invented your provenance, inscribed you, authenticated you, and vouched for you?[/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: from Tang Bohu's schoolmates, to Yixing's modern notables, to the master artisans of New China,
Late Ming Courtesan: generation after generation!?
([u]Ke Xiang[/u] lowers her eyes to the Late Ming Courtesan. 
When has she come to stand behind the Late Ming Courtesan?
[u]Ke's[/u] arms hanging quietly at her sides.)
Late Ming Courtesan: Even if you are genuine... So what!
Late Ming Courtesan: By what right does a vessel shaped by an artisan endure at least as legend for hundreds of years,
Late Ming Courtesan: while an evening of my labour, into which I poured my youth, my skill, and my whole heart,
Late Ming Courtesan: [b]perishes with the banquet, leaving only a few stray lines written down by who knows whom?[/b]
Late Ming Courtesan: A scholar hands me lyrics that fit neither melody nor rhyme.
Late Ming Courtesan: I sing them to the tune, labouring over the words, correcting the cadence and finding the proper rhymes.
Late Ming Courtesan: And afterwards the songs enter his collected verses, without so much as my name.
Late Ming Courtesan: Gongchun! How I envy you!
(She springs to her feet.)
Late Ming Courtesan: [b]Even at times when [i]wine-red stains my crimson skirt[/i], I grieve that my gifts have gone unrecognised![/b]
([u]Baochai[/u] walks over to the Late Ming Courtesan, slowly, tentatively resting a hand on her shoulder.
[u]Ke Xiang[/u] looks deeply at the Late Ming Courtesan. 
Her parted lips trembling ever so slightly, her large eyes filled with emotions so complex.
[u]Lv Meng[/u] takes two steps toward the Late Ming Courtesan, hesitates, then gently nudges "Hermes" forward. 
The [u]Late Ming Courtesan[/u] gradually composes herself. 
[u]She[/u] gives Baochai's hand a gentle pat, then turns to look Ke Xiang straight in the eyes.
The [u]Late Ming Courtesan[/u]'s fingers brush over the thick patch of matching fabric sewn onto the shoulder of Ke Xiang's jacket.
It is a little trick Ke Xiang devised to keep the carrying pole from digging into her bones.
The three settle into their seats. [u]"Hermes"[/u] tugs Lv Meng down into a seat beside her.)
"Bilbo": But... my dear child, 
"Bilbo": without those gentry and literati, purple clay teapot culture—
"Bilbo": and, well, that sort of culture—
"Bilbo": [n]wouldn't it have lost its most important patrons?[/n]
"Bilbo": You know, with cultural things like these, their worth is rarely just the clay and the wages.
"Bilbo": They rely on people appreciating them, buying them, 
"Bilbo": and passing them back and forth in fellowship to gather all that extra meaning.
"Bilbo": One buys a teapot for a friend, or leaves it to one's children and grandchildren.
"Bilbo": Think of all the affection, all the stories that travel down the generations with it.
"Bilbo": [b]Those bonds of affection, the good customs of villages, those webs of human warmth...[/b] 
"Bilbo": [b]that have grown up around them,[/b]
"Bilbo": [b]and how everyone in that network pictures one another and themselves...[/b]
"Bilbo": Those bonds and the cultural meaning behind a gift were always meant to nourish each other, weren't they?
"Bilbo": Like the mithril coat Thorin gave me. Without him, how should it ever have become so precious to me?
"Bilbo": Though as for... the kind of "culture" you spoke of...
([u]"Bilbo"[/u] cannot bring himself to look at the Late Ming Courtesan. 
He glances at "I", then jerks his gaze away as though he'd been given an electric shock.)
"Bilbo": I have never, er, been a patron of it myself... 
"Bilbo": For Ilúvatar's sake! There are children present!
"Bilbo": What I mean is, I'd like to believe that among those people, 
"Bilbo": there must have been some who were genuinely gentle and kind, who knew how to cherish others...
(Bilbo stops.)
(Baochai and Lv Meng look at the Late Ming Courtesan without speaking.)
Ke Xiang: Blood and kinship, neighbours and friends
Ke Xiang: these bonds need not be lies.
Ke Xiang: [b]To observe and criticise is not to bury every human kindness before our eyes.[/b]
“Hermes”: But that's a form of structural oppression,
“Hermes”: not caused by one or two wicked squires, 
“Hermes”: and not undone by a handful of kindhearted nice guys either.
“Hermes”: Inside a structure like that, even the oppressed can turn around and wound someone else.
“Hermes”: What calls for reflection is the structure of social relations,
“Hermes”: [b]and the ways objects, through the roles, practices, and norms they imply,[/b]
“Hermes”: [b]help those relations unfold and reproduce themselves.[/b]
“I”: (I give Hermes a nudge.) You're stealing somebody else's lines, aren't you? (Winks at Kexiang, can't hold back smiles)
Ke Xiang: (A calm, steady gaze) Some well-worn lessons you've heard so often. 
Ke Xiang: Your ears have grown calluses from hearing every line. 
Ke Xiang: Without fresh observations, what new understanding can you gain?
Ke Xiang: [b]If life has taught you nothing new, why ask me to teach it all again?[/b]
(I can no longer bring myself to smile.)
<<set $MindDone4 to true>>
<<mindmaze_reset_hands>>
<<jump MindMaze_Continue>>
===


